ument is that the document whose main  thesis is right you will recall that  Muslims and Christians what binds them  is not just belief in common God in one  God but also commitment to worship God  and also to love to love neighbor to  love God and to love to love neighbor  now what's what's interesting here is  that commonalities significant  commonalities are affirmed without  denying the differences differences are  clearly preserved but there is an  indication of a significant overlap in  in core convictions of these two  tradition and obviously both of these  traditions get the idea of love of  neighbor love of God and love of  neighbor from Judaism and also that  communities are something that touches  what is genuinely at the heart of these  faiths because you can imagine how some  third party might find it convenient to  find convergences between faith and then  pull things together so as to create  something artificially as overlapping  right and then obviously it will become  the productive because nobody would be  embracing that particular faith what's  interesting here is that at least that's  the claim that the commonality is  touched what each of these faiths deep  down embrace as part of genuinely their  own and also I think what is significant  is that what's common  is love of God and love of neighbor now  it could be nasty right if the common  thing was something something rather  negative in regards to exclusivity or  something of that something of that sort  so a commonality that an overlap is a  love of God and love of the neighbor  which takes us i think to an important  aspect of our discussion here and namely  that in discussion about faith and  reconciliation and violence it is really  significant to look at the concrete  shapes of faith you can just say faith  but you have to look at the content of  fades and many of the fates Judaism  Christianity and Islam certainly have  elements in their traditions which do  not seem particularly conduc